  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 240 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nAs the shortwave trough passes through the CWA Friday night, ridging \nsets back in over the Central Plains as a high pressure slides in \nalong the Iowa-Missouri border behind a weak cold front on Saturday. \nWesterly flow will take hold in the low levels, limiting our cold \nair advection. Highs are forecast to jump into the mid-to- \nupper 80s on Saturday and climb further into early next week. \nAnother ridge-riding shortwave is expected to deepen a new low \npressure center over south-central Canada on Sunday and \nintroduce southerly flow back to the area. Combining the low- \nlevel moisture advection and peak evapotranspiration from the \ncrops, dew points will likely (60-80%) soar back into the \nmid-70s. Current LREF guidance has 40-70% probabilities for \nheat indices exceeding 100 degrees, mainly along and south of \nInterstate 80.\n\nHeading into Monday, a cold front pushes southward into Minnesota, \nnorthern Iowa, and Wisconsin, increasing PoPs (10-20% coverage) along\na thin axis of mid-level moisture. Highs and dew points will be\nsimilar to Sunday, but additional cloud cover could help lessen\nheat impacts. By Monday night, the cold front should clear most\nof the CWA as high pressure over Minnesota advects in some \ncooler, drier air from the north for Tuesday and Wednesday. \nHighs in the low-to-mid 90s on Monday (50-70% chance) should \nslowly sink into the upper 80s by Wednesday as dew points fall \nback into the mid-to-upper 60s (50-80% chance).\n\nContinual ridging over the South-Central Plains is expected to limit \nany significant cooldown during the middle of next week, but gradual \nretrograding of the ridge to the southwest should open the door to \nadditional trough passages late next week.",
